Inside the eval call, the model may generate JavaScript roughly like:

const tickets = [
    "I cannot log in after changing my password this morning.",
    "Our production dashboard has stopped showing new customer data.",
    "Please tell me how I can download last month's invoice."
];

const results = await Promise.all(
    tickets.map((ticket, index) =>
        task({
            description:
                `Analyse Ticket ${index + 1}: ${ticket}. ` +
                `Return priority, issue, and recommended action.`,
            subagentType: "ticket-analyst"
        })
    )
);

results;



We do not write that JavaScript ourself in the Python program.
The main model writes it dynamically inside the interpreter based on the number and shape of tickets.
That is the central idea of this lesson.
The current task() interpreter primitive takes a description and subagentType, runs a full subagent loop,
and returns the subagent result.


